    "result_text": "All tests pass (the suite is the single acceptance test, now green). \n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the soft-duplicate rule for `POST /api/owners`: a new owner that isn't a hard duplicate but shares an existing owner's **lastName + postcode** with a **different telephone** is still created and flagged.\n\n**New logic (mirrors the existing `Namesake`/`CountNamesakes` pattern):**\n- `PossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 the single matching predicate: same last name (case-insensitive), same non-blank postcode, different telephone.\n- `FlagPossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 pipeline step that scans owners, and records the earliest-created match's id in `possibleDuplicateOf` (unset when none). Placed right after `EnsureUniqueIdentity`, so hard duplicates are already rejected and any match here is genuinely soft.\n\n**Wiring & persistence:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `flagPossibleDuplicate` step under the same `transaction` governance.\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added the persisted `possibleDuplicateOf` (Integer) column + accessors.\n- `schema.sql` (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres) \u2014 added `possible_duplicate_of INTEGER`.\n\n**Response contract:**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added read-only `possibleDuplicate` (boolean) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (int32) to the `Owner` schema.\n- `OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 `possibleDuplicateOf` maps by name; `possibleDuplicate` is derived (`possibleDuplicateOf != null`), so there's one source of truth rather than a redundant stored flag.\n\nThe value is fixed at creation and returned unchanged on later `GET`s, consistent with `namesakeCount` and the other derived fields.",
